@node CMacs PARA
@chapter CMacs PARA: the shared notes taxonomy

PARA --- Projects, Areas, Resources, Archives, plus an Inbox --- is how
the notes tree is organised, and more than one subsystem needs to agree
about it: @ref{CMacs Roamgraph} colours nodes by bucket, and
@ref{CMacs Secondbrain} groups its Memory ring by department.

Two independent answers to ``which bucket is this file in?'' would
disagree at the edges, so there is one, in @file{lisp/cmacs/cmacs-para.el}.

It also resolves a live bug.  @code{cmacs-roamgraph-directory} defaulted
to @code{org-roam-directory} (often @file{~/org}) while
@code{cmacs-brigade-memory-roots} defaulted to @file{~/Documents/notes}.
On a default install that made PARA colouring @emph{silently inert} ---
no error, just every node the fallback colour.  Both now consult
@code{cmacs-para-roots}.

@section The shape

@example
00_inbox/          unprocessed captures
01_projects/       time-bounded work with an outcome
02_areas/          ongoing responsibilities
03_resources/      topic reference
04_archives/       inactive, preserving the original path
@end example

Most top levels carry @file{personal/} and @file{work/} beneath them, and
each directory has a @file{00_index.org}.

@section API

@example
(cmacs-para-classify "~/Documents/notes/01_projects/work/x.org")
;; => (:category projects :dir "01_projects" :scope work
;;     :special nil :bucket "01_projects" :rel "01_projects/work/x.org")

(cmacs-para-bucket FILE)      ; the grouping string a colour keys on
(cmacs-para-color BUCKET)     ; 0xRRGGBBAA
(cmacs-para-root-for FILE)    ; which root contains it
(cmacs-para-relative FILE)    ; FILE relative to that root
@end example

@code{cmacs-para-roots} is a list, because a file is classified against
whichever root contains it and there is no reason to assume one.  The
@emph{longest} matching root wins, so a tree nested inside another still
classifies correctly.

@section Two refinements over a naive split

Both exist because the naive version produced a worse map.

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{@file{02_areas/dailies} is its own bucket.}  It is roughly 40% of
a mature tree, and folded into areas it swamps that colour entirely.
@item
@strong{Scope and role are reported alongside the category}, not folded
into it, so a caller can group by whichever it wants.  Archives mirror
the original tree, so scope sits one level deeper there --- reading the
first component after @file{04_archives} would report @code{nil} for
every archived file.
@end itemize

A path outside every root classifies as @code{nil} rather than guessing,
and an unrecognised bucket still gets a colour rather than @code{nil}: a
file outside the trees must still render.

@section Colours

Fixed, not hashed, so a colour means the same thing in every session ---
the only way they become readable at a glance.  Inbox amber, projects
blue, areas green, dailies violet, resources yellow, archives grey.

@section Tests

@file{test/cmacs/cmacs-para-tests.el}, 11 tests, no display and no
filesystem access --- classification works on the path string.  Covers
every category, the longest-root rule, the dailies split, personal/work
scope, archives keeping their inner scope, index files, colour
distinctness, and that roamgraph genuinely delegates here rather than
keeping a second copy.
